WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:09.710
In this video, we'll start looking at how we can create assets like levels, static meshes, and materials inside of Unreal Engine.

2
00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:13.710
Okay, so now let's look at creating a new level inside of Unreal Engine.

3
00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:20.710
So similar to Unity, we can go up to the File menu, and instead of hitting New Scene, we can hit New Level.

4
00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:24.710
This will open up a couple of templates that we can pick from.

5
00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:25.710
Select Open World.

6
00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:30.710
Additionally, we can right-click in the content drawer and create a new level from here.

7
00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:37.740
Next, we'll create an actor, as opposed to Unity, which has the new Game Object button as part of the hierarchy.

8
00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:43.740
Over in Unreal Engine, we have the new Actor button as part of the toolbar.

9
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:49.740
Here, I will go to Shapes and Cube and drag it into my level.

10
00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:53.740
I can also press F to hone in on it and see it down here.

11
00:00:53.740 --> 00:01:00.740
We can see that this cube has a transform and a static mesh component and a material component.

12
00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:15.090
This is going to be very similar to a game object inside of Unity that has a transform component, a mesh filter, a mesh renderer, and a box collider, as well as a rigid body.

13
00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:18.090
Next, we're going to add a component to our new actor.

14
00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:26.090
This would be very similar to how we would do it in Unity, where we hit this Add Component button and select from our list of components.

15
00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:32.090
In Unreal, we can select our cube and hit the Add Component button up here at the top.

16
00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:36.090
In this case, I'm going to look for a rotating movement component.

17
00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:39.090
Take off that one.

18
00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.090
I think this is another one.

19
00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:49.090
One thing to note here, if we want this static mesh to actually be able to move, we have to set it to movable here.

20
00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:59.090
This is important for how Unreal Engine allocates objects for rendering so that movable objects can be rendered together and static objects can be rendered together.

21
00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:04.090
Now, when we press Play, we can see our lovely box spinning in place for us.

22
00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:08.719
Now, let's move on to creating a Blueprint.

23
00:02:08.719 --> 00:02:13.719
This is how we're going to be able to add interactivity back to our content.

24
00:02:13.719 --> 00:02:19.719
Entire games can actually be made using Blueprint visual scripting without even touching C++.

25
00:02:19.719 --> 00:02:28.330
So, back over here in Unreal, I can right-click, create a Blueprint class.

26
00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:36.389
We're going to pick an actor, and I will call this BP underscore spin cube.

27
00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:42.340
We'll open this up. We see our Blueprint editor.

28
00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:45.340
We see our default scene route.

29
00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:50.340
And let's go ahead and add a static mesh component.

30
00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:55.550
This has a transform, and we can assign a static mesh here.

31
00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.710
We will look for cube.

32
00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:03.689
We'll hit compile.

33
00:03:03.689 --> 00:03:06.689
If we zoom out, there's our cube here.

34
00:03:06.689 --> 00:03:09.689
We have a default material for now.

35
00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:12.689
And then we see a couple tabs up here.

36
00:03:12.689 --> 00:03:18.689
We have the construction tab that we won't look at, this construction graph, and our event graph.

37
00:03:18.689 --> 00:03:26.689
So, instead of needing to write a C sharp component over here in Unity and add...

38
00:03:26.689 --> 00:03:37.069
Let's see, we have an axis rotation component that I've written that will allow us to spin around our cube here in Unity.

39
00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:42.169
Back over in Unreal, if we want to do the same thing,

40
00:03:42.169 --> 00:03:49.460
once again, I can add my rotating movement component.

41
00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:54.939
I can drag that into the graph to get a reference to it.

42
00:03:54.939 --> 00:04:00.939
And I can get the pull-off from this pin here,

43
00:04:00.939 --> 00:04:07.939
and this will bring up all of the different properties that we might want to look for

44
00:04:07.939 --> 00:04:11.000
using the rotating movement component.

45
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.150
I'm going to get its rotation rate.

46
00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:18.470
Let's see, there we go, get rotation rate.

47
00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:25.069
I want to multiply this by negative one.

48
00:04:25.069 --> 00:04:31.709
And then I want to set the rotation rate.

49
00:04:31.709 --> 00:04:39.709
And so what this is going to allow me to do is change the direction that the cube is spinning in.

50
00:04:39.709 --> 00:04:45.449
However, right now, these nodes here will never run

51
00:04:45.449 --> 00:04:50.449
because they aren't hooked up to an event that will trigger them.

52
00:04:50.449 --> 00:04:54.449
So, I'm going to delete both of these to give myself a little more room.

53
00:04:54.449 --> 00:04:57.449
I'm going to search for the space bar.

54
00:04:57.449 --> 00:05:09.829
We'll drag that up here, drag this back over, get ourselves a little more organized here.

55
00:05:09.829 --> 00:05:18.879
And now, when we press the space bar, we are going to set our rotation rate.

56
00:05:18.879 --> 00:05:23.040
However, if we try to do this immediately right now,

57
00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.040
let's see if we drag in our spin cube.

58
00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:27.040
Ooh, it's a little bit bigger, isn't it?

59
00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.139
And we hit play.

60
00:05:29.139 --> 00:05:34.139
We'll see that it spins, but when we hit space bar, nothing happens except for our character moving up.

61
00:05:34.139 --> 00:05:36.199
Why is that?

62
00:05:36.199 --> 00:05:38.199
So, how do we enable input?

63
00:05:38.199 --> 00:05:40.420
Well, why don't we search for that?

64
00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:42.420
Enable input.

65
00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:47.420
And one thing we need for this to work here is a reference to the player controller.

66
00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:53.420
We'll talk about this a little bit more when we go into the gameplay framework inside of Unreal Engine.

67
00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:57.420
But now, I'm going to hit compile this.

68
00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:05.610
We should see, we go to test, our cube is spinning, and we press space, and it turns the other way.

69
00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:09.779
Amazing.

70
00:06:09.779 --> 00:06:14.779
One more thing that we can do here in order to make our code a little more reusable

71
00:06:14.779 --> 00:06:21.779
is what if we one day wanted to call this logic without pressing the space bar?

72
00:06:21.779 --> 00:06:27.939
Well, in order to do that, we would need to create our own custom event

73
00:06:27.939 --> 00:06:37.350
to switch direction and have our logic to change the rotation direction

74
00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:40.350
listen for that instead.

75
00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:48.779
Now, our space bar can call switch direction.

76
00:06:48.779 --> 00:06:51.779
We'll save this again here. We'll compile this up here.

77
00:06:51.779 --> 00:06:59.779
So, similar to when you save a C Sharp script and it compiles before you can add it to your game objects.

78
00:06:59.779 --> 00:07:04.779
This is after we make changes. We want Unreal Engine to compile our blueprint code.

79
00:07:04.779 --> 00:07:06.839
I'll save it as well.

80
00:07:06.839 --> 00:07:12.129
And now, just to double check that it works, press space, and it spins around.

81
00:07:12.129 --> 00:07:20.310
Okay, so now we'll start to change the look of our actors, and we'll do that by creating a material.

82
00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:28.310
This is a look at the material graph, which looks a lot like the blueprint editor's graph.

83
00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:38.310
We'll be dropping nodes onto here and using that to create logic that will affect the rendering properties of our material.

84
00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:48.720
So, we can get started by right-clicking, creating material, and I'll call this M underscore cube.

85
00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:56.850
And if you want to see how to more consistently name your assets throughout your project and across projects,

86
00:07:56.850 --> 00:08:02.850
I would recommend checking out the Michael Auer style guide.

87
00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:08.850
This is a wide array of generally accepted nomenclature.

88
00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:13.850
So, when you start working with more developers or looking at other people's projects,

89
00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:18.550
you'll be able to better understand what's inside of them.

90
00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:27.550
So, here we have our basic output mode that's on the surface material domain and has a blend mode of opaque.

91
00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:34.549
And with the default lit shading model, we won't worry too much about all that.

92
00:08:34.549 --> 00:08:39.549
What we want to do is add a constant vector three.

93
00:08:39.549 --> 00:08:48.549
So, if you don't know, this vector can also be a color representation.

94
00:08:48.549 --> 00:08:53.710
So, I'm going to pick a color that I like, this color purple.

95
00:08:53.710 --> 00:09:00.779
Okay, and we can drag that into the base color output, and we'll see our preview over here start to change.

96
00:09:00.779 --> 00:09:03.779
And I'll drag it into the emissive color output.

97
00:09:03.779 --> 00:09:05.779
And now it's starting to glow.

98
00:09:05.779 --> 00:09:11.779
We'll hit apply. This is very similar to the compile button inside of a blueprint editor.

99
00:09:11.779 --> 00:09:23.340
And then now over here in our level, we can drag our material onto, where's our static mesh?

100
00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:26.340
There we go. And we'll see when we pick up the material to drag it in,

101
00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:33.340
it highlights the area that it is applicable or possible to be dragged into.

102
00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:37.340
And there we go. We've got our new material on our cube.

103
00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:40.340
One thing we'll notice, we have a little asterisk here next to our level.

104
00:09:40.340 --> 00:09:42.340
That means we aren't saved.

105
00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:44.340
So, let's go ahead and click save.

106
00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:51.500
And I'll call this one map session level.

107
00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:54.940
And I'll save that here in the session content folder.

108
00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:57.299
There we go. Awesome.

109
00:09:57.299 --> 00:10:02.330
Now when we press play, we can see our material in action.

110
00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:08.220
So, the next thing we're going to make is a material instance.

111
00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:15.220
This is a great thing to get your head around early in your Unreal development journey.

112
00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:20.220
Material instances are a way to take a single material asset

113
00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:22.220
and make it more extendable and reusable

114
00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:29.220
and reduce your total memory footprint of assets inside of your game.

115
00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:32.220
So, what we can do by setting up our base material

116
00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:36.220
that we just created with different parameters

117
00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:38.220
is that we can then edit those parameters

118
00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:42.220
in different instances of that material.

119
00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:44.220
This gives us a lot more flexibility

120
00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:47.220
and then reduces our memory footprint

121
00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:50.220
and lets our game run better.

122
00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:52.220
So, how are we going to do that?

123
00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:58.019
Back over here in Unreal, we can right click on our material

124
00:10:58.019 --> 00:11:03.019
and right up here at the top we see create material instance.

125
00:11:03.019 --> 00:11:09.970
The nomenclature for this one is going to be MI

126
00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:11.970
and we can open this up.

127
00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:13.970
And so far we see we've got some parameters here

128
00:11:13.970 --> 00:11:17.970
but nothing that we can change for our color.

129
00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:19.970
So, how can we do that?

130
00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:21.970
Well, if we come back over to our base material

131
00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:27.029
and we right click, we'll see this option to convert to parameter.

132
00:11:27.029 --> 00:11:30.029
This gives us an opportunity to name it

133
00:11:30.029 --> 00:11:35.029
and then we also see it appear over here in the details panel.

134
00:11:35.029 --> 00:11:39.250
Hit apply, save it too just for good measure

135
00:11:39.250 --> 00:11:42.250
and now here in our material instance

136
00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:46.250
we see that we have a global vector parameter for color

137
00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:53.250
that we can override and make this one a fun green color for instance.

138
00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:56.379
We'll hit save, go back to our session level,

139
00:11:56.379 --> 00:12:00.379
select our cube instance and we can drag our material instance

140
00:12:00.379 --> 00:12:04.379
into our cube and now we have two different cubes

141
00:12:04.379 --> 00:12:08.379
but they both share the same core asset for their material.

142
00:12:08.379 --> 00:12:14.460
In Unity, this direct relationship between a material

143
00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:19.460
and a material instance doesn't exist in separate assets.

144
00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:26.460
Although you can instance objects that have the same material on them

145
00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:30.200
and so we just saw this over in editor

146
00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:32.200
but after you've created a material instance

147
00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:36.200
you can assign it in the material slot of a static mesh

148
00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:40.200
the same way you can with a master or base material

149
00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:45.350
and it should be noted that you should really try and rely on this workflow

150
00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:50.350
as much as you can in order to reduce the total amount of memory

151
00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:54.350
that your project takes up and game takes up.

152
00:12:56.149 --> 00:12:59.149
In the next video, we'll look at how we can add content

153
00:12:59.149 --> 00:13:04.149
to our Unreal Engine project and then use it in our levels.
